<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Talas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/751">751</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Talas River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Talas was fought in 751 near the Talas River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, where a Tang army was defeated by an Abbasid-allied force. It is often cited as a marker of the limit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> expansion westward. Tradition also links the battle to the spread of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">paper-making</a> to the Islamic world through captured artisans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas.jpg" alt="Battle of Talas" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Talas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and the Arab caliphates had met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> since the early eighth century. In 715 a Tang force defeated a Tibetan-Arab army at the Battle of Namangan and restored the king of Fergana, and in 717 Tang troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksu">Aksu</a>. In 747 the Tang general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-xianzhi">Gao Xianzhi</a> established control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgit">Gilgit</a> region, and in 750 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> took power after the fall of the Umayyad Caliphate.</p><p>The immediate cause was a quarrel over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Shash</a>, the region of modern Tashkent. Gao Xianzhi led Tang and Karluk soldiers against its king, who surrendered and was taken to the Tang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> and executed. His son then appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, and the Abbasid commander Ziyad ibn Salih received reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> in May 751.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 751 the armies met on the Talas River, near the present border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, though the exact site is unconfirmed. According to the Chinese account, the two sides fought to a stalemate for five days, with crossbowmen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> behind a shield wall of infantry. On the fifth day the Karluk mercenaries, who made up two thirds of the Tang army, attacked it from the rear while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> attacked from the front.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-xianzhi">Gao Xianzhi</a> escaped with some of his regulars, helped by his officer Li Siye, but most of his troops were captured. Numbers are uncertain. Chinese sources put the Tang dead at 20,000 to 30,000, and Arabic sources at 45,000 to 50,000 with 20,000 to 25,000 captured. Sources also differ on whether the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karluks">Karluks</a> changed sides during the battle or were Abbasid allies from the start.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Historians judge the battle differently. One view holds that it ended Tang influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and secured Abbasid control of the region and the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a>. Another holds that it was little more than a border clash: borders barely moved, Arab envoys visited the Tang court four times in 752 and 753, and the Tang kept considerable influence in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rebellion of 755 forced them to withdraw troops from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anxi-protectorate">Protectorate General to Pacify the West</a>.</p><p>Chinese prisoners taken at Talas are said to have introduced papermaking to West Asia, but several findings dispute this. The Arabic historian al-Maqdisi reports that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-muslim">Abu Muslim</a> took 5,000 Chinese prisoners. The battle is recorded in both Chinese and Arabic sources, which differ in their accounts of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Talas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Battle of Talas</b> in 751, where a Tang army under Gao Xianzhi was defeated by Abbasid forces after the defection of its Karluk allies, is often cited as marking the limit of Tang westward expansion, and is traditionally linked with the transmission of paper-making westward through captured…</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Bayanchur Khan the khaganate brought the Sekiz Oghuz, Kyrgyz, Karluks, Türgesh, Toquz Tatars and Chiks under its rule, and after the Tang defeat by the Arabs at the <b>Battle of Talas</b> in 751 the Uyghurs emerged as the dominant power of Central Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Turks first met Islam at the <b>Battle of Talas</b> and converted largely through Sufism; in the 14th century Özbeg Khan of the Golden Horde turned to Islam, and the Turkic and Mongolic peoples of Central Asia largely converted.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven west by the Uyghur Khaganate, they moved to the Zhetysu region, and in 751 their defection to the Abbasid side helped decide the <b>Battle of Talas</b> against Tang China.</blockquote><a href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluk Yabgu</a></li><li><blockquote>In 750 the Tang general Gao Xianzhi executed the ruler of Chach, whose son sought help from the Abbasids; the resulting <b>Battle of Talas</b> in 751 ended Chinese influence west of the Tian Shan.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-chach">Principality of Chach</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferghana was contested between the Umayyads, the Turgesh Khaganate and Tang China until after the <b>Battle of Talas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Principality of Ferghana</a></li></ul></section>
